# Avenlyo Hetzner staging reverse proxy.
#
# Caddy is the only internet-facing process (ports 80/443) and the only container that peers with
# `api` at all -- deploy/compose.yaml puts `web` and `api` on separate networks precisely so that
# the API's trusted-proxy boundary is structurally one hop rather than "any private container".
# Upstreams are reached by Docker's internal DNS (`web`, `api` are the compose service names), not
# 127.0.0.1, which from inside this container would loop back to Caddy itself.
#
# TLS is fully automatic: Caddy requests and renews certificates via ACME (Let's Encrypt) the
# moment DNS for these two hostnames resolves to this host and 80/443 are reachable. No certificate
# file is ever committed to this repository, and no Certbot or other separate ACME client is used.

# Forwarding information is REPLACED, never appended to.
#
# Caddy's default is to append the connecting address to whatever X-Forwarded-For arrived, so an
# internet client can prepend entries of its choosing and they survive the hop. The API decides a
# caller's abuse-control identity from this header, and a caller writing its own value there is a
# caller choosing its own rate-limit bucket -- or someone else's. `{remote_host}` is the address
# Caddy actually accepted the connection from, so anything the client wrote is discarded at the one
# hop that knows the truth.
#
# The API does not depend on this being correct: apps/api/src/security/client-identity.ts honours a
# forwarding header only from an internal peer, and the network split means the only such peer is
# this process. Two independent controls, either sufficient.
(sanitized_forwarding) {
	header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
	header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
}

staging.avenlyo.com {
	reverse_proxy web:3000 {
		import sanitized_forwarding
	}
}

api-staging.avenlyo.com {
	reverse_proxy api:4000 {
		import sanitized_forwarding
	}
}

# Internal-only listener for the web container's own server-side API calls.
#
# `web` no longer shares a network with `api`, so `AVENLYO_API_URL=http://api:4000` cannot resolve.
# The dashboard's appointment, billing and integration server actions still need to reach the API,
# and routing them out to the public hostname and back would add a TLS hairpin plus a dependency on
# public DNS from inside the container for what is a purely internal call.
#
# So they go through Caddy, which is already the one process allowed to talk to `api`. This port is
# NOT in the `ports:` block of deploy/compose.yaml, so it is reachable only from the two compose
# networks -- never from the host or the internet. No hostname is matched here, so it never triggers
# an ACME request.
:8080 {
	reverse_proxy api:4000 {
		import sanitized_forwarding
	}
}
